---
// The footer every page ends in, written once.
//
// Measured on the day it was written, over the 44 hand-written fragments: 44
// footers, 42 of them distinct, and every one the same three parts in the same
// order. Zero, one or two paragraphs that belong to the page (which file its
// blocks are cut from, which edit produced its diagnostic), then the byline,
// then the copyright line. The byline had already drifted while it was copied:
// three wordings in Italian (the hand-written pages, the examples, the
// specification page) and two in English, none of them decided, each the
// residue of whichever pass last touched that file. BaseLayout kept the footers
// in the pages until a build comparison could check the extraction rather than
// trust it; this is that extraction, and the comparison is in the commit that
// landed it. Author instruction, 2026-09-08: the footer was repeated on every
// page, and Astro can do better than that.
//
// The page's own paragraphs arrive through the slot. BaseLayout cuts them out
// of the rendered page (site/src/lib/footer.ts) and passes them here, so a
// fragment still ends in the <footer> a reader expects and carries nothing
// that is shared.
//
// The byline is a FRAGMENT per edition rather than markup in this file, for
// the reason i18n.ts holds nothing long: the net's `records/english` reads
// every .astro file and admits Italian prose only under site/src/html/it/. A
// paragraph of Italian here would be the first outside the edition, and the
// check would be right to refuse it. The copyright line is in neither language
// and sits here.
//
// **The copyright line opens with the release**, `heroes X.Y.Z`, on every page
// of both editions (author instruction 2026-11-03, meant as: *I want the
// released version number to appear on the site too*; the footer was the
// author's choice over the nav, whose row is measured to the pixel). It is
// `version()`, read from `VERSION` in `selfhost/main.hero`, which moves only
// in the commit that carries the tag `vX.Y.Z`, so the number is the newest
// release's and cannot disagree with `heroes --version`; it is in neither
// language, like the rest of the line. It links to the tag's page on GitHub,
// which answers as soon as the tag is pushed, before the CI has written the
// release's notes into it (measured that day: a tag with no release answers
// 200, a tag that does not exist 404), so the push that carries the commit
// carries the tag with it. `target` and `rel` are written by hand for the
// reason `SiteNav.astro` gives: a component never passes through `page()`.
import bylineEn from '../html/_byline.html?raw';
import bylineIt from '../html/it/_byline.html?raw';
import { version } from '../lib/tables.ts';

interface Props {
  /** The URL this page answers at, absolute, ending in `/`. */
  url: string;
}

const { url } = Astro.props;

const lang = url.startsWith('/it/') || url === '/it/' ? 'it' : 'en';
const byline = (lang === 'it' ? bylineIt : bylineEn).trim();
const release = version();
---
<footer>
  <slot />
  <Fragment set:html={byline} />
  <p><a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href={`https://github.com/heroes-lang/heroes/releases/tag/v${release}`}>heroes {release}</a> &middot; &copy; 2026 Giuseppe Arici &middot; heroes-lang.org</p>
</footer>
